WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.279
You can't outrun destiny — and the destiny
for Netflix's The Witcher is another season.

00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:09.090
Based on Polish author Andrzej Sapkowski's
Witcher novels and shorts that were turned

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:13.690
into a video game series by CD Projekt Red
, The Witcher was already lined up for a second

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:17.720
season prior to its official debut on December
20, 2019.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:21.940
The action-fantasy show, created by Lauren
Schmidt Hissrich, takes place in a medieval

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:26.020
world within a realm called the Continent,
and follows the lives of three unique but

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:27.340
linked-together characters.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:31.119
Throughout the first season's eight episodes,
viewers saw how the trio were molded by their

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:35.670
individual experiences before meeting one
another and The Witcher season 1 finale concluded

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:36.980
with Ciri asking Geralt,

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:38.040
"Who is Yennefer?"

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.120
With The Witcher season 1 ending on that delicious,
cliffhanger-y note, fans are understandably

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.449
foaming at the mouth for any and all information
about season 2.

00:00:46.449 --> 00:00:50.269
Though details are sparse for the time being,
here's everything we know about the second

00:00:50.269 --> 00:00:51.200
season of The Witcher.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.860
The Witcher season 2 doesn't have a confirmed
release date as of December 2019.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:00.160
However, when the series' renewal was announced,
it was reported that production on the new

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.500
season will begin in London in early 2020
in preparation for a 2021 launch date.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.530
It wasn't specified when in 2021 The Witcher
season 2 might premiere, but it's most plausible

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:13.800
that fans won't be seeing more of the show
until the middle of that year at the very

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:14.380
earliest.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.020
That said, the production timeline for The
Witcher season 1 shows a two-year-and-seven-month

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:24.030
gap between when the project was confirmed
in May 2017 to when it premiered on Netflix

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.680
in December 2019.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:29.800
While it certainly won't be that long of a
wait for The Witcher season 2, if production

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:35.390
is slated to begin around February or March
2020, fans could be looking at a summer 2021

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:40.140
debut date unless Netflix allots the cast,
crew, and creatives more resources and funding

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.240
to speed things up.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:45.920
it's a given that the major players from season
1 will be back for the series' second go-around

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:46.660
on Netflix.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.220
The trio's individual paths cross at the end
of season 1 of The Witcher, and in Sapkowski's

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:55.040
written works, they experience many trials,
tribulations, and triumphs together after

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:55.540
meeting.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:56.860
"What, you know this woman?

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.100
Of course you know this woman."

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.620
When Netflix confirmed that The Witcher would
receive a second season on the streamer, creator-showrunner

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:07.770
Lauren Schmidt Hissrich noted in a press release
that the show's team was keen to, quote, "continue

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.900
telling the stories of Geralt, Yennefer, and Ciri."

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:14.670
That doesn't give us much to go on, but given
that season 1 pulls from Sapkowski's The Last

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:19.530
Wish and Sword of Destiny, it stands to reason
that season 2 of The Witcher will focus on

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:25.720
the first few books of the main Witcher saga:
1994's Blood of Elves, 1995's Time of Contempt,

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:28.300
and/or 1996's Baptism of Fire.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.170
But we're getting ahead of ourselves here.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:34.020
The truth of the matter is that no one involved
with The Witcher has said anything explicit

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.820
about the plot of the series' forthcoming
second season.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:41.040
Speaking with CinemaBlend, Hissrich teased
that The Witcher season 2 will only expand

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.340
on the characters and plots showcased on season
1, saying,

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:48.640
"[Season 1], of course, carried a lot of weight
because you're setting up the entire world.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:52.099
You need to set up all of the characters,
you need to set up the politics, you need

00:02:52.099 --> 00:02:56.090
to start to understand what a witcher is and
what they do and how that's changing in the

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:57.200
dynamics of the world.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:00.820
The first season carries a lot of weight,
but there's a lot of things that we did this

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:04.580
season to set up events that we know we want
to happen in season 2 and beyond."

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.260
In a separate conversation with GamesRadar,
Hissrich doubled down on these comments, saying

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:12.859
that, quote, "all of the relationships that
we've been setting up in season 1 actually

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:15.000
start to come into fruition in season 2."

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.220
She further noted,

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:20.100
"What's great about season 2, I can tell you,
is that, in what we've written, the story

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:21.620
becomes much more focused.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:26.349
There's a stronger drive in the story [...] Characters
start meeting and interacting more.

00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:27.540
That goes well sometimes.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.440
It doesn't go well sometimes.

00:03:29.809 --> 00:03:33.099
But it's kind of like, all of those building
blocks that we set up for the world, finally

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:36.139
start to come together into something a little
more concrete."

00:03:36.139 --> 00:03:40.280
Hissrich has also said that she has seven
seasons of the series mapped out, so there's

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:44.860
much, much more Witcher goodness to come...
if Netflix chooses to make it so.

